sri lanka has received 787 million  dollars from the international monetary  fund and 150 million dollars from the  bangladesh central bank this was all  part of an effort to boost the  struggling nation's foreign exchange  reserves which have been depleted as it  struggles to recover from the  coronavirus pandemic the kind of funding  that the imf has sent to the island is  called a special drawing rights it's a  special kind of international reserve  asset  and is very very rarely used  last in 2009  now sri lankan president gotabaya  rajapaska declared a state of emergency  over food shortages as banks run out of  foreign exchange to finance imports he  named a top army officer as commissioner  general of essential services to  coordinate the limited supply of paddy  rice sugar and other groceries long  queues have formed outside stores and  supermarkets many shelves are already  empty milk powder and cooking oil are  virtually impossible to get  people have also had to wait for hours  to fill their motorbikes rickshaws and  cars with gasoline the energy minister  has appealed to motorists to use fuel  sparingly warning that it could be  rationed by the end of the year unless  consumption was reduced the asian  country is suffering from the economic  fallout of the pandemic the economy in  sri lanka shrank 3.6 last year  and even though growth has returned the  money shortage means that the government  cannot even buy enough medicine  to supply hospitals  earlier i spoke to ganesh mortim an  economist at the university of colombo  i asked him what factors had sparked  this crisis  a foreign issue is one thing because sri  lanka imports  some food items like sugar and  other essential items  on top of that sri lanka entire depend  on imports for its gasoline need and  also gas so  the shortages are now  in  gas and petrol that's sometimes back uh  the shortages are there because of these  uh shortages in foreign currencies so  this country's foreign reserves have  been depleted to the alarming level so  the government could not maintain its  import flow into the country so there  was a  serious uh  import control regime has been  in place put in place  so  the local  production of rice and other commodities  vegetables and other commodities uh uh  basically we have seen uh there is no no  reason for the shortage as of now  um the especially the rice it has been  produced adequately  but  we can't see them in the in the in the  market i think in adequate quantities so  something went wrong  somewhere so the  emergency regulation  is intended to bring this rice into the  market i think  and  how have these food shortages been  impacting people in sri lanka so far  uh indeed the food shortage has  pushed these prices prices of essential  commodities uh for example i can give  you some figures on rice  it was earlier sold at  0.35  euro cents  to can make a comparison and now it is  0.5 euro cents so for a  ordinary family that's a huge increase  in the stable food price so it's it's  very difficult for ordinary family to  get ordinary rice not only rice all of  the commodities we have seen that the  prices are now climbing up you know  disproportionately so uh it's for our  ordinary family it's very difficult to  manage with the situation  ganesha morty m from the university of  colombo there thank you for talking to  us  in the middle of sri lanka's economic  crisis comes a new initiative by the  government to improve the country's  biggest export tea  but what was meant well might push the  entire industry in danger  the soil on this sunny plantation  produces one of the best teas in the  world  as do neighboring plantations all across  sri lanka  tea connoisseurs appreciate the  country's sale on tea for its color and  taste  tea is sri lanka's main export bringing  in more than one and a quarter billion  dollars a year and accounting for about  10 percent of the country's export  income but president kotabaya  rajapaksa's recent ban on chemical  fertilizers has growers worried their  plants depend on the nutrients they are  fed  it is the urea and the nitrogen content  that in that  forces the growth of the tea party of  the bud  and of the green leaf without it  you can  you can expect the decline in production  by as much as 50 percent  rajapaksa's idea was to make sale on tea  entirely organic and ready for a fast  growing market but tea growers worry  about their harvest  and about the economic consequences of a  failed policy which would go far beyond  the loss of income  to number one  make a big dent in the foreign exchange  earnings of the country number two  it is going to render a great part of  our population unemployed  three million people in sri lanka work  as tea pickers add to that workers on  cinnamon and pepper plantations that are  expected to face similar problems if the  fertilizer ban is not lifted and time is  of the essence  our chemical fertilizers reserves will  last about two months maximum after that  we need replenishments if we don't get  that in time there will be a very  drastic drop in crop of tea leaves  but right now president raja paksa  doesn't seem to be willing to change  course he's betting on improved  long-term food security for sri lankans  hopes the tea growers aren't sharing
 

